options.explanation:
Saying "$y$ is an implicit function of $x$" means the same as saying that $y(x)$ is a function whose equation is unknown.

The derivative of $y(x)$ is $y'(x)$, or $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.

We treat any expression that contains $y$ as a composite function, so differentiating it involves the chain rule.

For example, this is how we differentiate the term $2y^2$ (from our equation) implicitly:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}(2y^2)&=2\dfrac{d}{dx}[y(x)]^2&&\gray{\text{Write }y\text{ as }y(x)}
\\\\
&=2\cdot 2[y(x)]\cdot y'(x)&&\gray{\text{Chain rule}}
\\\\
&=4y\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}&&\gray{\text{Go back to }y\text{ and }\dfrac{dy}{dx}}
\end{align}$

hints.content:
Now we can plug the point $(0,-1)$ into the expression for $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\dfrac{y-3x^2}{4y-x}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(-1)-3(0)^2}{4(-1)-0}\qquad\gray{x=0,\,\,y=-1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-1}{-4}
\\\\
&=\dfrac14
\end{align}$
